WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.810
We are now ready to develop
a Spectral Alignment Algorithm, and

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.540
we will capitalize on the observation
that a modification corresponds to either

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:18.020
block insertion, or
block deletion in the Peptide vector.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:25.170
And, therefore, maybe we can us
an analogy with sequence alignment,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.610
where "alignment" corresponds to
the minimum of insertion, deletion, and

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.450
substitutions of amino acids.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:36.410
So, our goal is to figure out

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:42.690
whether we can find similarities between
Peptide vector, and Spectrum vector

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.970
by finding a longest path in a properly
constructed spectral alignment graph.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.090
The only question is
how to construct this graph.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:52.610
Let's start.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.102
In the beginning, let's construct

00:00:58.102 --> 00:01:02.646
first a graph that I call
Southeast(m,m+delta),

00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:07.332
which consists of a grid

00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:12.599
with m+1 rows and m+delta+1 columns.

00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:17.419
Edges in this graph will
simply correspond to all edges

00:01:17.419 --> 00:01:20.576
going in the Southeast direction.

00:01:20.576 --> 00:01:25.772
And given that peptide a_1,...,a_m of mass m

00:01:25.772 --> 00:01:30.672
and its modified variant peptide^mod
a'_1,...,a'_n

00:01:30.672 --> 00:01:35.585
of mass m+delta,

00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:39.661
we can construct a path formed by this Peptide,

00:01:39.661 --> 00:01:44.508
Peptide and Peptide',
in the graph Southeast(m,m+delta),

00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:49.040
and such path is shown in this graph.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:55.957
So this blue path corresponds
to a modified Peptide with

00:01:55.957 --> 00:01:59.867
two modifications, shown in blue here,

00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:03.464
and this red path corresponds to a Peptide

00:02:03.464 --> 00:02:07.380
with three modifications,
shown in red here.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:13.930
So far, I failed to explain how
to construct a path between

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:19.660
an unmodified Peptide, in green here, and
its modified version, shown in blue here.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.270
Let me do this now.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.710
So this path will start
at the the node (0,0),

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.040
then move to the node with the coordinate

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.630
equal to the masses of
prefixes of length 1 of this Peptide,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:42.700
then move to the coordinate corresponding
to masses of prefixes of length 2,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:48.460
and finally, move to the node with
coordinate corresponding to

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:54.880
the masses of the entire unmodified
and modified Peptides.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.792
We will also distinguish between two
types of edges in the resulting graph.

00:03:00.792 --> 00:03:04.910
The ones that are showed by dashed edges

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.790
are non-diagonal edges, and these
are edges corresponding to modifications.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.440
They are non-diagonal because
modification has happened.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.840
And this is an example of a diagonal edge.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.344
There is no modification, and the masses
of amino acids in the unmodified Peptide

00:03:23.344 --> 00:03:26.200
and in modified Peptide are the same.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.655
So, we will consider different
paths in the Southeast(m,m+delta)

00:03:32.655 --> 00:03:37.382
and, in fact,
any Peptide with mass m+delta

00:03:37.382 --> 00:03:41.720
will correspond to a path
in the Southeast graph.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:50.130
Moreover, we can substitute these paths
by a Spectral vector, with mass m+delta.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:55.595
And therefore, any possible interpretation
of a Spectral vector with mass

00:03:55.595 --> 00:04:01.000
m+delta will correspond
to a path in this graph.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.870
Therefore, I will rename
this Southeast graph

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:11.710
into the graph that is denoted
Southeast(Peptide,Spectrum),

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:17.940
and moreover, I will remove all light rows
from this graph, because I don't need them.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.200
All paths corresponding to a
pair of unmodified and

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.630
modified Peptides traverse
only dark rows in this graph.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.726
And therefore,
all light rows can be safely removed.

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:39.970
The resulting graph will be called
PSMGraph(Peptide,Spectrum),

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:45.580
which is defined as the graph
where the weights of all

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:53.000
nodes in column i are equal to the i-th
coordinate in the Spectrum vector,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.421
and the rows in this graph,
it's important to note,

00:04:58.421 --> 00:05:02.758
have indices not equal to 0, 1, 2, 3,

00:05:02.758 --> 00:05:06.973
but equal to 0, mass(a1), mass(a1a2), ..., and

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:11.810
masses of prefixes of
the unmodified Peptide.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:19.390
And, in this formulation,
the score between the modified Peptide,

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:25.330
and Spectrum is simply the weight
of the path for the modified Peptide.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.510
And, therefore, we have reduced
the spectral alignment problem

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:35.410
to the following problem in the PSMGraph:

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:39.350
Given PSMGraph(Peptide,Spectrum)
and an integer k,

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.100
we want to find a maximum-weight
path in this graph

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.210
with at most k non-diagonal edges.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.890
Well, we already know how to search for
a maximum-weight path.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:58.200
But how would you search for
a maximum-weight path

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:05.460
with an additional constraint that
it has at most k non-diagonal edges.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:10.145
To find an optimal path in
the PSMGraph with an additional

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:15.060
constraints on the number
of non-diagonal edges k,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.600
we will construct yet

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.750
another graph, which is called
the Spectral Alignment Graph.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:24.490
This graph will have k+1 layers,

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:29.859
and each layer will be
identical to the PSMGraph.

00:06:29.859 --> 00:06:34.183
Weights of nodes in
the Spectral Alignment Graph,

00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:39.321
will be simply inherited from
the nodes in the PSMGraph.

00:06:39.321 --> 00:06:43.224
Now, this graph on the right
side of the slide,

00:06:43.224 --> 00:06:48.240
Spectrum Alignment Graph,
is currently disconnected.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.600
There are no edges connecting the
different layers of this graph.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.260
We will connect layers of
this graph in the following way:

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.730
Diagonal edges from the PSMGraph
will stay on the same layer.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:08.800
For example, the blue edge stays
on the same layer in this case.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.170
But non-diagonal edges from the PSMGraph
will move to the next layer,

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:19.000
like in the case of the non-diagonal
red edge, on the right of the slide.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:27.520
Now, for the case of the next pair
of edges, the same rule applies.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:34.050
And finally, for every path in
the original PSMGraph, we will have

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:39.720
a path in the Spectral Alignment Graph
going through multiple layers,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.270
and the spectral alignment
problem is now reformulated as

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:49.100
finding paths in the k-layer
Spectral Alignment Graph,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:53.400
and our goal is to find
a maximum-weight path in this graph.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:59.650
Before you implement
the Spectral Alignment challenge,

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.820
let me clarify some
implementation details for you.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.560
I will introduce the notion
of the diff array.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.330
The diff array will simply represent
differences in the indexes

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.230
of consecutive rows in the PSMGraph.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:21.570
For example,
diff(2) = 2-0 = 2

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:26.870
diff(5) = 5-2 = 3

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:33.490
And here, all the elements
of the diff array are presented.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:40.796
Now, to solve a longest path problem
in the spectral alignment graph,

00:08:40.796 --> 00:08:44.812
we will define the variable score(i,j,t),

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:50.169
which is the maximum weight
of paths from (0,0,0),

00:08:50.169 --> 00:08:54.608
the source of this
graph, to the node (i,j,t).

00:08:54.608 --> 00:09:02.360
And node (i,j,t) has just one
predecessor in the same layer,

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:08.510
and we can compute the coordinates
of this node using the diff array.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.530
And it has j predecessors
in the previous layer, and

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:18.890
we also can compute the coordinates
of these nodes using the diff array.

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:23.900
Therefore, score(i,j,k)
can be defined as the

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:30.910
s_j + maximum

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:36.350
over all predecessors,
score(predecessor)

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:39.840
To be more precise, we will use

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.140
the diff array to specify the exact
element of this recurrence.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:51.830
And, of course, the maximum score of all
Peptide with at most k modifications

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:59.520
is simply the maximum value
of score(m,m+delta,t) for

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:06.700
all possible t, represented by
green nodes in this graph.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:12.840
After you learned about so many algorithms
from computational proteomics,

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.540
the time has come to address
the challenge problem for this chapter.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:24.920
In the same paper where Azara presented T-Rex peptides,
they also presented mastodon peptides, and

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:29.820
we want you to re-analyze them from the
perspective of statistical significance.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.280
It actually has never been done.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.770
There is nothing surprising about
finding collagen peptides in mastodons

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.810
because mastodons roamed the Earth,
just 10,000 years ago.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:46.400
They went extinct because
of the climate change and

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:52.180
over-hunting by humans armed
with stone weapons.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:57.523
So, we want you to analyze the mastodon
peptides reported in this paper

00:10:57.523 --> 00:11:02.510
and decide which of them
are statistically significant.

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:07.453
We want you do find other statistically
significant mastodon peptides

00:11:07.453 --> 00:11:09.771
that this paper possibly missed.

00:11:09.771 --> 00:11:14.265
And we want you to find
hemoglobin peptides, if you can,

00:11:14.265 --> 00:11:17.205
matching spectra from mastodons.

